# Removing "(age X)" from a String in JavaScript

Here are several ways to remove the "(age X)" pattern from your string, where X can be any number:

## Method 1: Using Regular Expression (Recommended)

```javascript
const str = "july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)";
const result = str.replace(/\s*\(age \d+\)\s*/, ' ').trim();

console.log(result);
// Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

## Method 2: More Flexible Regex (handles multi-digit ages)

```javascript
const str = "july 7 1994 (age 25) London, UK (1994-07-07)";
const result = str.replace(/\(age\s+\d+\)/g, '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();

console.log(result);
// Output: "july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)"
```

## Method 3: As a Reusable Function

```javascript
function removeAge(text) {
  return text.replace(/\s*\(age\s+\d+\)\s*/gi, ' ').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
}

// Test with different ages
console.log(removeAge("july 7 1994 (age 7) London, UK (1994-07-07)"));
console.log(removeAge("january 15 1980 (age 43) New York (1980-01-15)"));
console.log(removeAge("march 3 2000 (age 100) Paris (2000-03-03)"));
```

## How the Regex Works

- `\(age` — Matches the literal text `(age`
- `\s+` — Matches one or more whitespace characters
- `\d+` — Matches **one or more digits** (so it works for any number: 7, 25, 100, etc.)
- `\)` — Matches the closing parenthesis
- `\s*` — Matches optional surrounding whitespace
- `gi` flag — Global (replaces all occurrences) and case-insensitive

## Output

```
july 7 1994 London, UK (1994-07-07)
```

The regex `\d+` ensures it works whether the age is `7`, `17`, `77`, or any other number. 🎯

